// Kernel operands are typed, not `dyn`: a component-wise operand is `Self`, a bulk one `List<Self>`.
//
// Every kernel parameter used to be `SigType::Dyn`, because the ABI could say "the implementor's own
// type" on the RETURN side (`RetTy::SameAsArg(0)`) and nowhere else. So `v.add(5)` type-checked
// clean and only failed once the kernel tried to zip a scalar against a byte buffer. `SigType::SelfTy`
// is the missing half of that vocabulary, resolved against the concrete implementor at the call site
// — which is why the diagnostic below can name `V3` and `List<V3>` rather than a trait parameter.
//
// `scale` is deliberately NOT `Self::Elem`: its factor is width-agnostic (the kernel converts), so a
// `Color` of `u8` scales by `2.0`. It declares `SigType::Numeric` — every numeric scalar and nothing
// else — so a whole vector is still refused.
// expect: error E0007 at 23:6
// expect: error E0007 at 24:6
// expect: error E0007 at 25:6
// expect: exit 1
use std.vec

@packed struct V3 { x: f32; y: f32; z: f32 }
impl vec.Kernels for V3 {}

a = V3 { x: 1.0f32, y: 2.0f32, z: 3.0f32 }

echo a.add(5)             // an int where the same shape is required
echo a.scale(a)           // a whole vector where a number is required
echo [a].add_all(a)       // a single vector where a list of them is required
